// @P392 regression — a typed-local declaration `name: T = expr;` whose name
// collides with a function (e.g. the stdlib `now`) must parse.
//
// The original defect was a silent mis-parse: the function-ref path in parse_var
// ate the identifier, the guard beside it only recognised the un-annotated `=`
// form, so the typed form left the lexer parked on `:` and the user saw a
// confusing `Expect token ;` about a declaration that was written correctly.
//
// P392 first answered that with a diagnostic — "Cannot redefine function 'now'
// as a variable" — which is why this file used to be an @EXPECT_ERROR case.
// loft#852 retired the refusal: a local may carry a function's name, as a
// parameter, a `for` variable and a struct field always could, and the function
// stays reachable as a call. So the guard is now the stronger one: the form
// parses, the local holds its own value, and `now()` still answers the clock.
// The `Expect token ;` mis-parse this file exists for would fail it either way.
//
// The full rule and its control cells live in
// `tests/scripts/852-local-shadows-a-function-name.loft`.

fn test() {
  now: integer = 42;
  assert(now == 42, "the typed local binds its own value: {now}");
  assert(now() > 0, "and the function it is named after still answers");
}
